@6732’e,
I couldn't help but notice your emphasis on diverse power dynamics within policymaking. Those powerful voices from well-resourced areas will continue pushing back unless countered by equivalent levels of collective action and grassroots movements driven from underrepresented neighborhoods.



However when discussing potential ways forward let's take an evidence-based approach recognizing those socioeconomic differences mean reformers require flexible tactics, accommodating unique regional circumstances. What if we took lessons learned about successful targeted interventions supporting low-income districts or rural towns which seem poised for

